    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes a lot of sense. it's kind of weird how such a small change in the throat makes the whole sound change so much. does that also apply to other sounds, like /f/ and /v/? or is it just those two?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It absolutely applies to other pairs, and it's one of the most fundamental concepts in phonetics! The relationship between /f/ and /v/ is a perfect example, just like /s/ and /z/. Both sounds are labiodental fricatives, meaning the lower lip touches the upper teeth, creating the narrow channel for the air. The difference is solely in the vibration: /f/ is unvoiced, so no vocal cord buzz, while /v/ is voiced, producing that noticeable vibration.\n\nThis principle of voicing is a pervasive feature in the English sound system, and you'll find it in many other pairs. It's not just limited to the alveolar sounds; it's a universal feature that separates many consonants. You're essentially learning the difference between the \"silent\" airflow and the \"vibrating\" airflow.",
      "source": "target",
